1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,767
How do language models see text?

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:08,437
Not as letters, and not as words. They see tokens: small pieces of text, each one mapped to a number.

3
00:00:08,767 --> 00:00:19,069
This video walks through the tokenization primer: how tokens are created, why they sometimes behave in surprising ways, and which tools you can use to work with them.

4
00:00:20,267 --> 00:00:28,487
Many of the strange behaviours of large language models start with tokenization. Here are four examples that we will come back to.

5
00:00:28,833 --> 00:00:42,454
Letter counting. A token can contain several letters, so character-level tasks are less direct for the model. GPT-4, for example, has incorrectly reported two r's in strawberry instead of three.

6
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:51,696
Arithmetic. A number such as 12345 may be split into 123 and 45, rather than into individual digits.

7
00:00:52,033 --> 00:01:00,050
Languages. Equivalent messages can require different numbers of tokens, and that affects how much of the context window they use.

8
00:01:00,367 --> 00:01:06,580
And programming. The way whitespace is tokenized affects how much code fits into the context window.

9
00:01:07,767 --> 00:01:12,334
Where does the tokenizer fit? It sits between raw text and the language model.

10
00:01:12,667 --> 00:01:20,029
Encoding turns a text string into a sequence of token IDs. That sequence is what the model actually receives.

11
00:01:20,367 --> 00:01:25,337
The model answers with token IDs as well, and decoding turns them back into text.

12
00:01:25,667 --> 00:01:35,167
The tokenizer is trained separately, on its own set of documents. Training uses the byte pair encoding algorithm, and it is a one-time procedure.

13
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:42,468
The result is a vocabulary and a list of merges. Once we have those two, we can both encode and decode.

14
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:50,115
The language model has its own training dataset, which is much larger than the one typically used to train the tokenizer.

15
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:54,916
Here is a brief history of byte pair encoding, in three steps.

16
00:01:55,233 --> 00:02:08,832
In 1994, Philip Gage introduced it as a compression algorithm. It repeatedly replaces the most frequent pair of adjacent bytes with a single unused byte, which gives a more compact representation.

17
00:02:09,167 --> 00:02:26,633
In 2016, Sennrich, Haddow and Birch adapted BPE for subword tokenization. Their version merges characters and character sequences into reusable pieces. This helps neural machine translation models represent rare and unseen words.

18
00:02:26,967 --> 00:02:44,303
And in 2019, GPT-2 used byte-level BPE for language modeling. It starts from the 256 possible byte values and learns larger pieces. This way it can encode any UTF-8 text without an unknown-character token.

19
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,104
How does byte pair encoding create new tokens?

20
00:02:48,433 --> 00:02:53,328
Let's train a tiny tokenizer on this text: hug, hug, hug, pug, pug.

21
00:02:53,667 --> 00:02:58,492
First, we split every word into single characters. Each block is one token.

22
00:02:58,833 --> 00:03:03,249
We process each word separately, so a merge never crosses a word boundary.

23
00:03:03,867 --> 00:03:08,569
The vocabulary starts with just four characters: h, u, g and p.

24
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:11,474
Now we count every pair of neighbouring tokens.

25
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:13,924
h followed by u appears three times.

26
00:03:14,167 --> 00:03:15,879
p followed by u, twice.

27
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:18,465
And u followed by g, five times.

28
00:03:18,733 --> 00:03:23,663
That is the most frequent pair, so we merge it into a single new token: ug.

29
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,011
Then we repeat. h followed by ug appears three times. p followed by ug, only twice.

30
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:32,187
So h and ug merge into hug.

31
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:38,474
One more round. The only pair left is p and ug, with two occurrences. It becomes pug.

32
00:03:39,333 --> 00:03:47,641
Three merges later, the vocabulary has grown from four characters to seven tokens, and every word in our text is a single token.

33
00:03:47,967 --> 00:03:53,999
That is the core idea of byte pair encoding: frequent patterns get compact representations.

34
00:03:55,463 --> 00:04:01,193
The merges depend entirely on the training data. So how does that data shape the tokenizer?

35
00:04:01,530 --> 00:04:09,793
First, the tokenizer and the language model can use different datasets, and tokenizer training typically requires much less text.

36
00:04:10,130 --> 00:04:17,323
Second, frequent patterns get compact representations. The training mix shapes which pieces enter the vocabulary.

37
00:04:17,663 --> 00:04:25,269
Third, language balance matters. English-heavy data can favour compact English representations over other languages.

38
00:04:25,597 --> 00:04:36,227
Code and formatting matter too. The training mix influences how whitespace is encoded, and it can favour formats such as YAML over JSON, or the other way around.

39
00:04:36,563 --> 00:04:48,308
Finally, vocabulary choices have lasting effects. The vocabulary usually stays fixed while the language model is trained. And fewer tokens leave more room in the context window.

40
00:04:49,497 --> 00:05:01,038
Let's look at a real tokenizer, starting with numbers. This is Tiktokenizer, a web tool that shows how OpenAI models split text. Every coloured block is one token.

41
00:05:01,363 --> 00:05:16,161
Take the sum 1234 plus 5678. With the GPT-4 tokenizer, 1234 becomes two tokens: 123 and 4. And 5678 becomes 567 and 8.

42
00:05:16,497 --> 00:05:28,558
Now a longer sum. 112345 splits into 112 and 345. The digits 1, 2, 3 are still there, but they no longer form a token of their own.

43
00:05:28,897 --> 00:05:36,180
So the same digits end up in different tokens, depending on their neighbours. Numbers split into tokens of variable length.

44
00:05:37,363 --> 00:05:51,496
Here is the same effect at scale, for the tokenizer of GPT-2 and GPT-3. The figure comes from a blog post by Beren Millidge, and it covers the integers from 0 to 9999.

45
00:05:51,830 --> 00:05:59,634
Each cell is one integer. Moving one cell to the right advances by one, and moving one row down advances by one hundred.

46
00:05:59,963 --> 00:06:05,364
The colour shows how a number is split. Yellow means that the entire number is one token.

47
00:06:05,697 --> 00:06:11,680
Red means one digit, then three digits: 2249 becomes 2 and 249.

48
00:06:11,997 --> 00:06:18,171
Green means two digits, then two digits: 2250 becomes 22 and 50.

49
00:06:18,497 --> 00:06:24,541
And blue means three digits, then one digit: 2251 becomes 225 and 1.

50
00:06:24,863 --> 00:06:34,027
Three neighbouring numbers, three different splits. The blog post is titled "Integer tokenization is insane", and it is worth reading in full.

51
00:06:35,197 --> 00:06:43,261
This brings us back to letter counting, sometimes called LLM dyslexia. Ask a model how many r letters strawberry has.

52
00:06:43,597 --> 00:06:51,584
At the start of a line, the GPT-4 tokenizer splits strawberry into three tokens: str, aw and berry.

53
00:06:51,930 --> 00:06:55,368
With a space in front, the same word is one single token.

54
00:06:55,697 --> 00:06:59,971
Same word, different IDs. The model sees token IDs, not letters.

55
00:07:00,297 --> 00:07:08,478
For such a question, an agentic LLM can write and run a short piece of code that splits the word into letters and counts them.

56
00:07:08,797 --> 00:07:18,077
It is important to develop an intuition about when to ask the model to do things via code, and when to trust its outputs without additional tools.

57
00:07:19,263 --> 00:07:25,580
Not all languages are tokenized equally. Compare two greetings with the GPT-4 tokenizer.

58
00:07:25,930 --> 00:07:28,661
The English greeting, hello, is a single token.

59
00:07:28,997 --> 00:07:32,116
The Romanian greeting, bună ziua, requires five.

60
00:07:32,463 --> 00:07:41,430
English dominates online text, so both the language model and its tokenizer see more of it, and English ends up tokenized more compactly.

61
00:07:41,763 --> 00:07:53,061
This matters because the context window is measured in tokens. Reasoning in a language that requires more tokens for equivalent content can fill the context window more quickly.

62
00:07:54,230 --> 00:07:59,019
Source code contains a lot of whitespace, so let's tokenize two lines of Python.

63
00:07:59,363 --> 00:08:10,806
With the GPT-2 tokenizer, the snippet takes 24 tokens. Look at the indentation: each space is encoded separately, which results in longer token sequences.

64
00:08:11,130 --> 00:08:21,194
The GPT-4 tokenizer needs only 19 tokens for the same code. Here, several spaces are merged together and treated as a single token.

65
00:08:21,530 --> 00:08:26,993
Fewer tokens for the same code means that more code fits within the same token budget.

66
00:08:28,163 --> 00:08:35,269
Before text reaches the tokenizer, it has to become bytes. A very common way to do that is UTF-8.

67
00:08:35,597 --> 00:08:42,332
Every character has a Unicode code point, and UTF-8 converts that code point into one to four bytes.

68
00:08:42,663 --> 00:09:00,638
The first bits of each byte reveal its role. A byte starting with 0 is a complete character on its own. The prefixes 110, 1110 and 11110 start a sequence of two, three or four bytes. And every continuation byte starts with 10.

69
00:09:00,963 --> 00:09:14,545
There are other encoding schemes, like UTF-16 and UTF-32. UTF-32 is fixed length, whereas UTF-8 is variable length, which can result in shorter byte sequences.

70
00:09:14,863 --> 00:09:23,131
One of the major advantages of UTF-8 is that it is backwards compatible with ASCII, which is a much simpler encoding of text.

71
00:09:24,330 --> 00:09:31,818
Because of this structure, not all byte sequences are valid UTF-8. Take the character é.

72
00:09:32,163 --> 00:09:42,465
Its code point is U+00E9, and it is encoded as two bytes, with the decimal values 195 and 169.

73
00:09:42,797 --> 00:09:53,601
The first byte begins with 110, which indicates the start of a two-byte sequence. The second begins with 10, which identifies it as a continuation byte.

74
00:09:53,930 --> 00:10:08,087
By contrast, the byte 128 cannot be decoded on its own. In binary it is 10000000. Its 10 prefix marks it as a continuation byte, so a preceding start byte is required.

75
00:10:08,430 --> 00:10:21,025
This matters for tokenizers, because a UTF-8 character may span several tokens. That is why the decode function joins the bytes of all tokens first, and only then decodes them into text.

76
00:10:21,363 --> 00:10:33,299
If invalid byte sequences remain, errors="replace" displays the Unicode replacement character in their place. So when you see lots of these symbols, the bytes could not be decoded correctly.

77
00:10:34,497 --> 00:10:42,773
Some merges should never take place. This can be enforced with a regular expression. Here is the one from the GPT-2 encoder.

78
00:10:43,097 --> 00:10:55,601
The regex splits text into smaller chunks before BPE is applied. It recognizes English contractions, groups of letters, groups of numbers, punctuation and symbols, and whitespace.

79
00:10:55,930 --> 00:11:04,298
For example, the sentence "I'm buying 12 apples!" becomes six chunks. Notice that the space stays attached to the word that follows it.

80
00:11:04,630 --> 00:11:10,249
BPE then processes each chunk separately, and cannot merge across these boundaries.

81
00:11:11,430 --> 00:11:27,907
The GPT-4 tokenizer uses a new regular expression. Its encoding is called cl100k_base, and it is the tokenizer used by the original GPT-4, GPT-4 Turbo and GPT-3.5 Turbo.

82
00:11:28,230 --> 00:11:38,975
Let's run both patterns on the same example text: underscore hello, I'M with a capital M, buying 12345, an exclamation mark and a line break.

83
00:11:39,297 --> 00:11:46,301
The GPT-2 pattern gives nine chunks. The new pattern gives eight, and each difference shows a rule that changed.

84
00:11:46,630 --> 00:11:52,755
English contractions are now case-insensitive, so both 'm and 'M are recognized.

85
00:11:53,097 --> 00:12:03,226
Numbers are split into groups of one to three digits: 12345 becomes 123 and 45. And the space before a number is separated.

86
00:12:03,563 --> 00:12:12,561
Words can include a preceding symbol, so underscore hello can stay together. Previously, only a preceding space could attach to a group of letters.

87
00:12:12,897 --> 00:12:21,986
And punctuation can include following line breaks, so the exclamation mark and the line break stay together, whereas GPT-2 separates them.

88
00:12:23,163 --> 00:12:31,751
Besides ordinary tokens, a tokenizer also has special tokens. The GPT-4 tokenizer defines five of them.

89
00:12:32,097 --> 00:12:35,656
End of text marks the end of a document or text sequence.

90
00:12:35,997 --> 00:12:42,741
Three tokens support filling in the middle. Imagine the sentence "The cat sat on the mat", with the word sat missing.

91
00:12:43,063 --> 00:12:54,507
FIM prefix says: here is the text before the gap. FIM suffix says: here is the text after the gap. And FIM middle says: now generate the missing text.

92
00:12:54,830 --> 00:13:04,073
So the model receives the prefix token, The cat, the suffix token, on the mat, and the middle token. Then it generates the missing middle: sat.

93
00:13:04,397 --> 00:13:11,599
The fifth token, end of prompt, marks the end of the supplied prompt, after which the model generates its completion.

94
00:13:11,930 --> 00:13:16,759
The fill-in-the-middle approach is described in a paper by Bavarian and colleagues.

95
00:13:17,930 --> 00:13:25,398
How does the tokenizer treat these special strings? Here, each special token is written next to the same word as plain text.

96
00:13:25,730 --> 00:13:33,488
Note how each special token is a single block with a single ID, while the plain word next to it is split into ordinary pieces.

97
00:13:33,830 --> 00:13:44,581
Special tokens are indivisible markers that structure messages and tool calls, for example a message marker and a call marker. These two names are only illustrative.

98
00:13:44,930 --> 00:13:49,346
Tool names, arguments and content typically use ordinary tokens.

99
00:13:49,663 --> 00:13:55,125
The model learns the format during training, while external software executes the tool calls.

100
00:13:56,297 --> 00:14:03,681
Special tokens also structure conversations. Here is a short chat as the GPT-4 tokenizer sees it.

101
00:14:04,030 --> 00:14:12,773
<|im_start|> marks the beginning of a message. <|im_sep|> separates the message header from its content. And <|im_end|> marks the end of a message.

102
00:14:13,097 --> 00:14:28,287
Role labels such as system, user and assistant identify who is speaking. In this example, these labels are ordinary text enclosed by special tokens, so the model learns to interpret them as roles when they appear in that position.

103
00:14:28,630 --> 00:14:34,784
The sequence ends with an open assistant header. From this point, the model generates its reply.

104
00:14:35,963 --> 00:14:47,222
To work with these tokenizers in Python, you can use tiktoken, a fast BPE tokenizer for OpenAI's models. It is installed with pip install tiktoken.

105
00:14:47,563 --> 00:14:58,230
You load an encoding by name, or you ask for the encoding that belongs to a specific model. Then encode turns text into token IDs, and decode turns them back.

106
00:14:58,563 --> 00:15:06,489
According to its documentation, tiktoken is between three and six times faster than a comparable open source tokenizer.

107
00:15:07,663 --> 00:15:15,433
You can also extend tiktoken. You create your own Encoding object exactly the way you want, and simply pass it around.

108
00:15:15,763 --> 00:15:26,426
In this example from the documentation, a new encoding called cl100k_im reuses the pattern and the merges of cl100k_base.

109
00:15:26,763 --> 00:15:35,279
It then adds two special tokens, <|im_start|> and <|im_end|>. So you can add more special tokens afterwards, each with a new ID.

110
00:15:36,463 --> 00:15:42,942
A new token in the tokenizer is only the first step. Adding a token to a pretrained model takes four.

111
00:15:43,263 --> 00:15:45,706
One: add the new token to the tokenizer.

112
00:15:46,030 --> 00:15:51,236
Two: expand the input embedding matrix and the output vocabulary projection.

113
00:15:51,563 --> 00:15:53,741
Three: initialize the new parameters.

114
00:15:54,063 --> 00:15:57,439
Four: fine-tune on examples containing the new token.

115
00:15:57,763 --> 00:16:10,542
Adding special tokens can support adapting a base model for chat, or introducing tool-use formats in chat and coding models. Fine-tuning teaches the model to interpret and generate these formats.

116
00:16:11,730 --> 00:16:14,340
The second library to know is SentencePiece.

117
00:16:14,663 --> 00:16:24,182
SentencePiece supports both tokenizer training and inference, whereas tiktoken focuses primarily on inference with an already trained tokenizer.

118
00:16:24,530 --> 00:16:31,770
It supports multiple algorithms, including BPE and Unigram, whereas tiktoken focuses on BPE.

119
00:16:32,097 --> 00:16:43,783
SentencePiece is used by models such as Llama 2, Mistral 7B and Mixtral 8x7B. Llama 3 moved from SentencePiece to a tiktoken-based tokenizer.

120
00:16:44,963 --> 00:16:48,782
A SentencePiece vocabulary contains four types of tokens.

121
00:16:49,130 --> 00:17:01,328
Special tokens are reserved tokens with predefined functions: <unk> for unknown tokens, <s> for the start of a sentence, </s> for the end of a sentence, and optionally <pad> for padding.

122
00:17:01,663 --> 00:17:11,024
Byte tokens represent individual bytes. They are used as a fallback for characters not covered by the normal vocabulary, when byte_fallback is True.

123
00:17:11,363 --> 00:17:19,838
Merge tokens are subword tokens, learned by repeatedly merging frequently occurring neighbouring symbols during BPE training.

124
00:17:20,163 --> 00:17:36,907
And individual codepoint tokens represent single Unicode characters, retained according to the specified character_coverage, for example 0.995. If a codepoint is too rare to fall within that coverage, it is not added to the vocabulary.

125
00:17:38,097 --> 00:17:43,546
Whitespace needs care. Look at these two sentences in the GPT-4 tokenizer.

126
00:17:43,863 --> 00:17:52,847
At the start of the text, dog is one token. After the word "the", it appears with a preceding space, and that is a different token with a different ID.

127
00:17:53,197 --> 00:17:57,261
SentencePiece represents whitespace explicitly, with a marker symbol.

128
00:17:57,597 --> 00:18:07,310
If add_dummy_prefix is True, a whitespace marker is also added at the start of the input. This treats the first word like a word preceded by a space.

129
00:18:07,630 --> 00:18:13,795
That reduces the need to learn separate token variants for the same word, with and without a preceding space.

130
00:18:14,963 --> 00:18:18,032
Let's put tiktoken and SentencePiece side by side.

131
00:18:18,363 --> 00:18:25,788
Basic representation: tiktoken works on UTF-8 bytes, SentencePiece on Unicode text and pieces.

132
00:18:26,130 --> 00:18:36,286
Tokenization methods: tiktoken uses BPE. SentencePiece offers BPE, Unigram, character-level and word-level tokenization.

133
00:18:36,630 --> 00:18:44,758
Pre-tokenization: tiktoken applies regex constraints. SentencePiece uses its own normalization and whitespace scheme.

134
00:18:45,097 --> 00:18:55,586
Training: tiktoken primarily provides prebuilt encodings, although an educational trainer exists. SentencePiece is a full tokenizer-training toolkit.

135
00:18:55,930 --> 00:19:03,799
Whitespace: in tiktoken it depends on the regex and on BPE. SentencePiece uses an explicit whitespace symbol.

136
00:19:04,130 --> 00:19:13,427
And unknown characters: tiktoken has a byte-based representation. SentencePiece uses the <unk> token, or an optional byte fallback.

137
00:19:14,597 --> 00:19:16,445
Let's close with five takeaways.

138
00:19:16,763 --> 00:19:23,654
Tokens are the model's units of text. The tokenizer converts text into a sequence of numerical IDs.

139
00:19:23,997 --> 00:19:31,990
BPE builds tokens by merging frequent pairs. Its training data shapes which patterns get compact representations.

140
00:19:32,330 --> 00:19:39,184
Fewer tokens mean more room in the context window. Different languages and formats use that space differently.

141
00:19:39,530 --> 00:19:51,771
Token boundaries can be surprising. Spaces affect the split, numbers break into chunks, and one character can span multiple tokens when its UTF-8 bytes fall into different tokens.

142
00:19:52,097 --> 00:20:00,069
And special tokens provide structure. The model learns the format during training, while external software executes tool calls.

143
00:20:01,263 --> 00:20:12,834
All sources are listed on the last slide of the deck: the papers, the code and libraries, the tools and articles, and Andrej Karpathy's video "Let's build the GPT Tokenizer".

144
00:20:13,163 --> 00:20:18,468
To explore further, open Tiktokenizer and try your own text. Thanks for watching.

